2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Er nevnt i 1444 og 1472.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ble født i Talgø i Ryfylke.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Til Talgø (Finnøy Prestegjeld i Ryfylke), Norges Riges Raad, nævnes i 1444 i en Granskning om Udlændingers Handel i Bergen; var endnu 1472 Væbner og sad da i Trondhjem som Meddommer i en Arvesag. Gift med Ingeborg, hvis Moder var 
2 CONC Ulvhilde Jonsdatter Smør.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bror til Jon Tordsson Benkestok - Lagrettemann i bergen 1435!
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 2 sønner
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT When TROND Tordsson Benkestok was a nobleman and married Birgitte (probably Torleivsdotter Kjællingmule) who was born in 1410 in Rogaland, Norway, his father, Tord, was 37 and his mother, Sigrid, was 10. He had one son with Bergiu
2 CONC th (Brynhild) Torleivsdatter Kjellingmule (Trondsson Benkestok) in 1450. He died in 1472 in his hometown at the age of 62.
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin of the family's name, allegedly meaning ‘tree-trunk seat’, has not been established. According to a myth, the family's founding father saved the King of Norway from Swedish soldiers by hiding him in a wooden bench, wher
2 CONC efore he was rewarded with noble status, name, and arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family's coat of arms is divided per pale. The dexter field has on a background Azure a half lily Argent. The sinister field is divided party per bend, where the lower part is Argent and the upper part is Azure. Being reminisc
2 CONC ent of a tree trunk, the latter is probably an allusion to the family name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trond Tordsson Benkestok of Talgøy was in 1444 mentioned as he attended the Council of the Kingdom. On 4 December, he took part in a council meeting in Copenhagen, where a ruling by King Christopher on the right of Hanseatic merch
2 CONC ants in Norway was confirmed. At the meeting, only twelve council members were present; five Lords of the Church and seven Lords of the Realm, of which Trond Benkestok was number six and with the title of knight. Trond, then in hi
2 CONC s late 20s, probably represented the Lord of Bohuslän, who was not present at the meeting. In 1472, he was still a knight and mentioned as a Judge of Peace at a court session in Trondheim concerning an inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later members of the family lived in Ryfylke, where Talgøy in Sjørnarøyane and Haraldseid at Skjold were their seat farms, as well as at Jordanger in Sogn. A family member moved north to Meløya Farm in Meløy, Nordland, which marke
2 CONC d the beginning of the expansion of the family in Northern Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trond Tordsson Benkestok of Talgøy was in 1444 mentioned as he attended the Council of the Kingdom. On 4 December, he took part in a council meeting in Copenhagen, where a ruling by King Christopher on the right of Hanseatic merch
2 CONC ants in Norway was confirmed. At the meeting, only twelve council members were present; five Lords of the Church and seven Lords of the Realm, of which Trond Benkestok was number six and with the title of knight. Trond, then in hi
2 CONC s late 20s, probably represented the Lord of Bohuslän, who was not present at the meeting. In 1472, he was still a knight and mentioned as a Judge of Peace at a court session in Trondheim concerning an inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later members of the family lived in Ryfylke, where Talgøy in Sjørnarøyane and Haraldseid at Skjold were their seat farms, as well as at Jordanger in Sogn. A family member moved north to Meløya Farm in Meløy, Nordland, which marke
2 CONC d the beginning of the expansion of the family in Northern Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1532, King Christian II was arrested and imprisoned in Copenhagen. Trond Torleivsson Benkestok actively supported Johan Kruckow, who wanted Frederik I on the Danish and the Norwegian throne. Trond was later a knight. Claus
2 CONC  Bille describes him as the ‘most respected and wise nobleman north of the mountains’. Trond was probably a supporter of the Roman Catholicism until the last Catholic archbishop, Olav Engelbrektsson, in 1537 fled the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jon Trondson Benkestok of Meløy (1530–ca. 1593), was a signatory when the Norwegian nobility in 1591 paid homage to King Christian IV at Akershus Castle. He signed the document using a signet ring with the family's arms.
