1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Oddernes Bygdebok B1, Gardshistorie side 652
2 CONT
2 CONT Mentioned in 1610 census
2 CONT
2 CONT http://digitalarkivet.no/cgi-win/WebCens.exe?slag=visbase&sidenr=29&filnamn=st10021610&gardpostnr=80&merk=80#ovre
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT google translation of the Norwegian information on this profile, source unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT A good part of his farm lay where Kristiansand is today. He defied the king's orders not to sell oak to foreigners. But in 1620 he was unlucky. He had sold to a Scotsman named Jørgen Pettersen of Manross. Then his ship was taken w
2 CONC hile it was at Kuholmen and sent down to Copenhagen. He was deprived of his half-living, and the landlord and 6 men showed up to record the property. In connection with the king buying land for the Kristiansand itself, Anders Olso
2 CONC n Eik had to give away parts (some hides) of Eg to the king against getting parts again. in Øvrebø (Øvrebø and Sangesland) and Klepland in Søgne. He did not even experience the city foundation itself. Olav's son moved to his fathe
2 CONC r's estate in Mosby, while Nils and Halvor drove Eg with his cousin Gunnar Paulsen (son of a brother of Anders). Later both Nils and Halvor came to Øvrebø.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to Eg, the Anders sons had 3 hides in Midhus Grim and 1 1/2 skin in Landås in Iveland. They had 2 hides in Highland on Lista. As a supplement to the city plot, his sons received 4 hides in Øvrebø in Øvrebø and 1 skin i
2 CONC n Sangesland in Øvrebø and 3 hides Klepland in Søgne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1645 Nils and Halvor Anderssønner EIK are owners of 3 hides in Øvrebø and Sangesland. In 1645, the same sons received a letter of parish from the parish priest Anders Nilsen at Oddernes on menswear (church property) in Øvrebø.
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm Eig (Eg) - From Oddernesboken by Kåre Rudjord: Where Kristiansand is today it was originally the two farms Grim and Eig, that is, the city center lies in what was the outfield for the farms. The border between the two far
2 CONC ms should have gone where Kirkegata is now. In total, the two farms covered the area between Topdalselva in the east, the sea in the south, Fiskå and Greipstad in the west and Prestøya in the north. These were very large farms tha
2 CONC t were originally part of Oddernes in Vest-Agder, but when Oddernes was split in two at the founding of Kristiansand, these two farms were incorporated into the city by forced change so the owners had to move to other farms. Since
2 CONC  the farms became part of Kristiansand, the author of the farm history for Oddernes has not mentioned these two farms other than that they are mentioned on a couple of pages.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons of Anders Olson Eik, Nils (ca 1618 - 1685) and Halvor Andersen Eig (ca 1622) and a cousin Gunnar Paulsen (ca 1590) ran the farm in 1641 when the farm was forcibly changed in connection with the Danish-Norwegian king Chris
2 CONC tian IV ( 1577-1648) decided that a city should be built here. Since the town was to be built in the outfield, the Eigs farmers hoped that they would keep the farm, but the King would not like it and in May 1642 they were told to 
2 CONC move. Unlike the owners of Grim, they objected to this, but after several subpoenas they in September 1643 "voluntarily" agreed to relocate. As remuneration for the change of husband they got land at Øvrebø and Sangesland in Øvreb
2 CONC ø and Vestre Klepland in Søgne, which was in the King's possession. According to Søgneboka, a few decades later Nils and Halvor Vestre Klepland sold to Klepland farmers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Died about 1641.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kilde:  http://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Eg-2
